  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nBy Wednesday, a quasi-omega block will develop with a high pressure \nsystem developing near/over the southern California coast and a low \npressure system over the Pacific and another low pressure system \ndraped across the eastern two-thirds of CONUS. Forecast \nconfidence is good that this pattern will hold through Thursday \nbefore it starts to break down. The high will strengthen slightly \non Wednesday, to around 591 dm across SoCal with H5 heights around\n587- 590 dm across southern AZ and SE CA. This will result in \ntemperatures increasing slightly, with Wednesday and Thursday \nforecasted to be the hottest days of the week. Afternoon high \ntemperatures are forecasted to be in the 100-108 degree range \nacross SE CA and SW AZ and in the 98-102 degree range across the \nlower deserts of south-central AZ. The afternoon high temperatures\nin the higher terrain areas in eastern Arizona will be in the mid\n80s to mid 90s. The combination of these high temperatures and \nlow temperatures around 10 degrees above normal will result in \nares of Moderate to Major HeatRisk, especially across SE CA and SW\nAZ. In fact, temperatures will be near record levels in El \nCentro, CA and Yuma, AZ. As a result, the Extreme Heat Watch \ncontinues for portions of western Imperial County, including the \nImperial Valley, and the Yuma area for Wednesday and Thursday. \nWith high pressure in place dry conditions are expected to persist\nwith no chances for rain, except for near the White Mtns \nWednesday with a weak shortwave moving through the Four Corners.\n\nThe high pressure to the west is then expected to start to weaken by \nthe end of the workweek and into the weekend with a trough moving \ninto western CONUS this upcoming weekend. However, forecast \nconfidence decreases considerably by the end of the weekend and \nstart of the following week as the ensemble spread is large with \nrespect to the timing of the high weakening and moving out of the \nregion and the trough moving in. The ensembles also disagree on the \nstrength of the trough for this upcoming weekend. Either way \ntemperatures are expected to gradually decrease back toward normal \nby the end of this weekend and beginning of next week. Additionally, \ndry conditions look to continue through at least Saturday, with \nslight chances for isolated showers and storms returning to the high \nterrain of eastern Arizona on Sunday. But, this could change \ndepending on the evolution of troughing during the weekend. Right \nnow better chances for rain look to arrive by the beginning of next \nweek, with the best chances across the higher terrain in eastern AZ. \nStay tuned for updates.",
